Let's formalize IOU update with maps.
Suppose lender L, borrower B, amount A.
We want final net debt between L and B:
Let x = amount L owes B (L.owes[B], B.owedBy[L])
Let y = amount B owes L (B.owes[L], L.owedBy[B])
After B borrows A from L, B's debt to L increases by A: y' = y + A.
But if x > 0, debts offset: net = y + A - x.
If net > 0: B owes L net. Set B.owes[L]=net, L.owedBy[B]=net; remove opposite.
If net < 0: L owes B -net. Set L.owes[B]=-net, B.owedBy[L]=-net; remove opposite.
If net == 0: remove both.
Our incremental algorithm:
existing = x.
if x > 0:
  offset = min(x, A)
  reduce L.owes[B] by offset, B.owedBy[L] by offset
  A -= offset
if A > 0:
  add to B.owes[L] A, L.owedBy[B] A
This works if y existing: add to y. If x > A, A becomes 0, L.owes[B] reduced to x-A. Good. If x < A, L.owes[B] removed, A residual added to B.owes[L] (which may have existing y, so y+A). Good. If x == A, removed. Good.
What if x and y both positive (shouldn't happen if normalized, but initial data could have both? User builder could set both owes and owedBy for same pair inconsistently. Tests likely consistent. If both positive, our 
algorithm offsets x first then adds to y, resulting y + A - x. It doesn't remove y if x > A? If x > A, y remains positive and L.owes[B] reduced, so both directions remain, not normalized. But initial data shouldn't have both. 
Could normalize pair fully to be safe.
Maybe better implement direct net calculation for pair to handle any initial both directions.
